  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 117 PM MST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nWarm and dry weather looks to continue for the forseeable future. On \nMonday, we will have a broad and fairly flat ridge positioned over \nthe region. A weak surface front will creep into the High Plains and \npush up against the Laramie range, keeping light winds in place over \nthe eastern areas, while those along and west of the Laramie range \nturn breezier. With 700-mb temperatures in the +4 to +7C range, we \nshould see continued above average temperatures and overall pleasant \nconditions. A weak and moisture starved clipper system will traverse \nnear the area on Tuesday into Wednesday. A few sprinkles or flurries \nwill be possible, but only a small minority of ensemble members \nsupport any precipitation whatsoever for the region. Expect this to \nknock 700-mb temperatures down to the 0 to +2C range, while pulling \nthe surface trough well east of the Laramie Range. This will allow \nbreezy conditions to spread into the High Plains. Temperatures are \nexpected to remain above seasonal averages, but with the cool down \nwe should be a little closer to normal (perhaps 5 to 8F above \naverage). While most will be breezy, high winds are fairly unlikely. \nIn-house guidance shows probabilities around 20 to 30% for the \ntypical wind prone areas. \n\nBehind the departing clipper system, ensembles are in excellent \nagreement showing a highly amplified ridge building in over the \nRockies Thursday into the following weekend. This feature will block \nor deflect any incoming storm systems, and push temperatures well \nabove average once again. Since we'll be on the periphery of this \nridge, temperature expectations are not too extreme. 700-mb \ntemperatures around +4 to +6C are currently shown in the NAEFS mean, \nwhich is near the climatological 90th percentile. This isn't quite \nwarm enough to support record highs, but expect temperatures at the \nsurface to range a good 10 to 15F above seasonal norms. The strong \nridge will likely also keep winds fairly light during this\nperiod.",
